      teach: [
        'Automatic capture beats manual logging for when and how long: machines record to the second, while people round, forget short stops and fill in the log at the end of the shift.\n\nPeople are still needed for why. A common set-up asks the operator for a reason only for stops above a threshold, say two minutes (a choice, not a standard). In data-quality terms: automatic capture fixes the completeness and accuracy of durations; the reason stays a human field.',
        'How the signal leaves the machine is covered in IT/OT on the shop floor (eng.ot). In short: newer machines offer OPC UA, the vendor-neutral standard (IEC 62541) that carries values together with their names, units and structure, with built-in security. Older machines often speak only Modbus, a simple protocol from the 1970s, or a proprietary one.\n\nAn edge gateway, a small industrial computer near the machines, reads the data, buffers it when the network drops, and forwards it.',
        'A machine with no data port can be retrofitted: a current clamp around the power cable, or a sensor on the stack light, the red, yellow and green signal tower, wired to an edge gateway. That gives running or stopped and a count, often for a few hundred euros per machine.\n\nThe catch: you must agree what each signal means. Does yellow mean “waiting for material” or “stopped”? Check it on the floor before anyone sees a number.',
        'The sampling rate, how often you take a value, must fit the signal. Machine states are best captured as events, the moment they change. Temperatures need a value every few seconds. Vibration needs thousands of samples per second: more than twice the highest frequency you want to see (the Nyquist criterion), and analysers typically use 2.56 times.\n\nSample too slowly and short stops disappear; sample everything too fast and storage fills with noise.',
        'Raw data needs cleaning: a counter rollover, where a counter passes its maximum and jumps back to 0; clocks that drift between systems; dead sensors that flatline; mixed units. Then comes contextualisation: adding which order, product and shift was running, because a stream of tag values, the named data points in a PLC, means nothing without it.\n\nThe question that exposes a weak setup: “What does ‘stopped’ mean in this signal, and how did you check it on the floor?”',
      ],
