      id: 'eng.industry40.predictive',
      title: 'Predictive maintenance and anomaly detection',
      pre: [
        {
          q: 'A pump has failed three times in five years. A supplier offers a model that predicts the pump’s remaining life from its sensor data. What is the main obstacle?',
          options: ['Pumps cannot be fitted with sensors', 'The model would need a faster computer', 'Three failures are too few to learn from', 'Remaining life cannot be measured in hours'],
          answer: 'Three failures are too few to learn from',
          why: 'Predicting remaining life is supervised: it needs many failures with the data leading up to them.',
        },
      ],
      teach: [
        'Maintenance strategies range from reactive, fixing it when it breaks, and preventive, servicing at fixed intervals, to condition-based maintenance: servicing when a measured condition, such as vibration or temperature, crosses a limit. Predictive maintenance goes one step further: data and models forecast when a failure will develop, so the repair can be planned.\n\nThe P-F curve and choosing a strategy per asset are covered in Maintenance (eng.maintenance).',
        'Anomaly detection is an unsupervised method: the model learns what normal looks like, such as the vibration pattern of a healthy compressor, and flags data that deviate. Because it needs only normal data, it works when failures are rare, which is almost always.\n\nThe flip side: an anomaly means “different”, not “broken”. A new product, a sensor that was moved or a cold morning can all look anomalous.',
        'Predicting remaining useful life, the time until failure, is supervised and needs many examples of failures with the data leading up to them. Few plants have that for one asset type, because they repair before failure.\n\nSo most predictive maintenance starts as condition monitoring with smart limits or anomaly detection, and grows into prediction where fleets of similar assets give enough failure data.',
        'Judge a model on two numbers. The detection rate: of the real developing faults, how many did it flag in time? Precision: of its alerts, how many were real? A model that flags everything has a perfect detection rate and terrible precision.\n\nThe warning must also come early enough: if spare parts take two weeks, a warning three days ahead saves little.',
        'What experts argue about: buy a vendor’s ready-made model, or build on your own data. Vendor models know the asset type; your data know your conditions. Many sites start with simple vibration and temperature limits on the bottleneck before any AI.\n\nTypical failure: sensors installed, alerts flowing, nobody planned to act. The sharp question: “When it flags, who checks, how fast, and how many weeks of warning do we need?”',
      ],
      example: {
        kind: 'worked',
        text: 'A food plant fits vibration sensors on 20 compressors and runs anomaly detection for a year. Results: 40 alerts, of which 10 were real developing faults. There were 12 real faults in total, so 2 were missed.\n\nStep 1, precision: 10 of 40 alerts were real, so 25%. Step 2, detection rate: 10 of 12 faults were flagged, so 83%. Step 3, value: each caught fault avoided an unplanned stop of about 6 hours; each false alert cost a technician one hour to check. Step 4: 25% precision is acceptable here, because a check is cheap and a stop is not. Step 5: tune to cut false alerts only if technicians start ignoring them.',
      },
